# description: Rename from a CSV file with two columns, old name and new name. Type the full path to the CSV file in the arguments. Kept as a learning example — the built-in CSV List Rename operation does this better.
# args: the full path to a CSV file

# Kept as a learning example only.
#
# Use the CSV List Rename OPERATION instead. It is better in every way —
# quoted fields, tab and custom separators, paths in the old column, tags in
# the new column — and this reproduces none of that, deliberately: the point of
# the example is the shape of a script, so it keeps the simplest possible
# limits:
#
#  * the separator is a hard-coded comma, with no quoting;
#  * only rows with exactly two fields are read, so a header line is skipped;
#  * on a duplicate old name the LAST match wins, because the loop has no
#    early exit. The native operation uses the first.
#
# It reads through fr.args_file(), which reads the path the *user* typed into
# the Arguments box. A script cannot name a different file.

state = {names: {}, rows: 0}

# --- init ------------------------------------------------------------------
text = fr.args_file()
for line in text.lines()
  fields = line.split(',').to_tuple()
  if (size fields) == 2
    # Last one wins: assigning unconditionally keeps scanning after a
    # match.
    state.names.insert fields[0], fields[1]
    state.rows += 1

rename = ||
  # An empty argument is a card nobody has filled in yet, not an error (P34).
  if fr.args.trim().is_empty()
    return ''
  # A file that read fine but had no usable rows is a different complaint from
  # one that could not be read at all, and saying the wrong one sends the user
  # looking in the wrong place.
  if state.rows == 0
    throw "no 'old,new' rows in '{fr.args}' — is the path right, and is it comma-separated?"
  found = state.names.get fr.filename
  if found == null then '' else found
